1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,996
One coding agent does one thing at a time.

2
00:00:03,296 --> 00:00:04,640
You ask, and you wait.

3
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:08,335
With symcc, you ask the lead in your chat.

4
00:00:08,635 --> 00:00:12,966
Here the owner wanted tasks that only
investigate, with nothing to merge.

5
00:00:14,016 --> 00:00:18,603
The lead chops the feature into small
tasks, and ties their order with red
threads.

6
00:00:18,903 --> 00:00:22,444
Several workers build side by side, each
in its own copy.

7
00:00:22,744 --> 00:00:24,643
Tests must pass before each merge.

8
00:00:24,943 --> 00:00:29,124
One task failed its tests six times, and
passed on the seventh try.

9
00:00:30,174 --> 00:00:33,246
But every change, even a good one, leaves
small holes.

10
00:00:33,546 --> 00:00:37,919
The new investigation tasks could lose a
question, or delete earlier work.

11
00:00:38,219 --> 00:00:39,947
Even a fix can open a new gap.

12
00:00:40,997 --> 00:00:42,981
That is why symcc has scouts.

13
00:00:43,281 --> 00:00:46,396
They wake on timers, read what changed,
and find the holes.

14
00:00:46,696 --> 00:00:50,600
Each finding becomes a task on the board,
marked with the scout's name.

15
00:00:51,650 --> 00:00:53,805
Those tasks go to workers like any other.

16
00:00:54,105 --> 00:00:55,427
Build, test, merge.

17
00:00:55,727 --> 00:00:57,455
The cracks close, one by one.

18
00:00:58,505 --> 00:01:02,495
Every task page shows where it came from,
its tries and its report.

19
00:01:02,795 --> 00:01:03,947
Nothing is lost.

20
00:01:04,997 --> 00:01:06,042
symcc.

21
00:01:06,342 --> 00:01:07,942
Many agents, one board.
